517
00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:28.010
And yeah, I was able to get through each phase,

518
00:20:28.010 --> 00:20:30.103
and Jason Camiolo.

519
00:20:30.950 --> 00:20:32.228
Who's right behind me right now.

520
00:20:32.228 --> 00:20:33.390
Who's right behind you.
He did it as well.

521
00:20:33.390 --> 00:20:35.530
So you guys were, I know you guys were on the blower

522
00:20:35.530 --> 00:20:36.850
with each other throughout the whole thing.

523
00:20:36.850 --> 00:20:39.620
Yeah, messaging after every single one.

524
00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:43.550
And I'd say like the second run,

525
00:20:43.550 --> 00:20:45.640
I stayed up till midnight the first day.

526
00:20:45.640 --> 00:20:46.920
Well, let's take a second and just,

527
00:20:46.920 --> 00:20:47.960
if somebody is brand new

528
00:20:47.960 --> 00:20:49.240
and they don't know what we're talking about,

529
00:20:49.240 --> 00:20:51.810
the Goggins challenge is this challenge

530
00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:55.010
that David Goggins proposed as kind of a crowdfunding,

531
00:20:55.010 --> 00:21:00.010
not crowdfunding, but a participatory challenge

532
00:21:00.150 --> 00:21:02.000
where you run four miles

533
00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:04.130
every four hours for 48 hours straight.

534
00:21:04.130 --> 00:21:07.307
It starts on a Friday night at eight o'clock, right?

535
00:21:07.307 --> 00:21:11.290
And you go through late Sunday afternoon basically.

536
00:21:11.290 --> 00:21:13.950
And so like the first run kind of,

537
00:21:13.950 --> 00:21:15.890
I was a little keyed up in my calf,

538
00:21:15.890 --> 00:21:18.550
my left calf was tightening up a little bit,

539
00:21:18.550 --> 00:21:20.610
but that was fine.

540
00:21:20.610 --> 00:21:23.250
I had a bit of an adrenaline crash after,

541
00:21:23.250 --> 00:21:25.380
and I didn't wanna sleep before the first midnight.

542
00:21:25.380 --> 00:21:27.500
I wanted to stretch and be ready.

543
00:21:27.500 --> 00:21:30.340
And so I didn't, slept, the second run was a lot better,

544
00:21:30.340 --> 00:21:32.510
slept a little bit after.

545
00:21:32.510 --> 00:21:33.900
And then it was like, you know,

546
00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:38.900
the 4:00 a.m. run, which was not very fast.

547
00:21:38.996 --> 00:21:42.460
(laughs) I was running past people who sleep in their vans.

548
00:21:42.460 --> 00:21:45.180
How much did you sleep before that run?

549
00:21:45.180 --> 00:21:47.697
Probably two and a half, three hours.

550
00:21:47.697 --> 00:21:48.530
That's pretty good.
That was probably

551
00:21:48.530 --> 00:21:51.310
the longest sleep of the entire situation.

552
00:21:51.310 --> 00:21:55.780
And then the fourth run, found a rhythm again.

553
00:21:55.780 --> 00:21:57.863
And I was listening to earbuds for the first time,

554
00:21:57.863 --> 00:22:00.010
that was 8:00 a.m. on Saturday.

555
00:22:00.010 --> 00:22:02.810
And I found that it kind of took away from the experience.

556
00:22:02.810 --> 00:22:04.340
Like I was, I thought I would

557
00:22:04.340 --> 00:22:06.390
get through all of Bravy or something,

558
00:22:06.390 --> 00:22:09.620
or like the whole, I was just gonna listen to books,

559
00:22:09.620 --> 00:22:12.030
but I found that it just wasn't working for me.

560
00:22:12.030 --> 00:22:14.220
Like I'd rather have the quiet.

561
00:22:14.220 --> 00:22:17.463
And so I stopped doing that.

562
00:22:18.340 --> 00:22:20.870
April ran two of the segments with me and she,

563
00:22:20.870 --> 00:22:22.310
usually I push the pram,

564
00:22:22.310 --> 00:22:24.210
but she was pushing the stroller the whole time.

565
00:22:24.210 --> 00:22:26.120
So she did, I think I pushed it

566
00:22:26.120 --> 00:22:27.680
for maybe half a mile at one point,

567
00:22:27.680 --> 00:22:31.170
but she did 16 of the miles.

568
00:22:31.170 --> 00:22:33.170
So she was with me and she rides.

569
00:22:33.170 --> 00:22:36.960
And the fastest of the weekend was that six run,

570
00:22:36.960 --> 00:22:38.913
the 20 to 24 miles.

571
00:22:39.910 --> 00:22:41.930
And then all of a sudden,

572
00:22:41.930 --> 00:22:43.690
people started calling me wanting to pace me.

573
00:22:43.690 --> 00:22:46.330
My buddy, Adam Dole calls me.

574
00:22:46.330 --> 00:22:48.200
Adam, early podcast guest.

575
00:22:48.200 --> 00:22:49.570
Early podcast guest.

576
00:22:49.570 --> 00:22:52.840
And shout out Adam Dole.

577
00:22:52.840 --> 00:22:54.340
And he called me like, hey, do you want,

578
00:22:54.340 --> 00:22:56.300
or texted me, do you want a pacer?

579
00:22:56.300 --> 00:22:57.820
It's okay if you say no.

580
00:22:57.820 --> 00:23:00.743
And I'm like, sure, at that point, I'll take any help.

581
00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:03.200
And so he came in.

582
00:23:03.200 --> 00:23:07.050
Another friend, Reece Pacheco who runs WSL PURE,

583
00:23:07.050 --> 00:23:10.550
the environmental organization, he came in for noon.

584
00:23:10.550 --> 00:23:13.440
And for the last day, Adam was at 8:00 p.m.,

585
00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:15.550
then he came back for 4:00 a.m.

586
00:23:15.550 --> 00:23:17.432
I mean, just incredible.
Wow, that's cool.

587
00:23:17.432 --> 00:23:20.100
And yeah, and so through it all,

588
00:23:20.100 --> 00:23:21.480
I didn't sleep much the second day.

589
00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:25.120
I slept after the 8:00 p.m. to midnight,

590
00:23:25.120 --> 00:23:26.490
and that was this craziest time,

591
00:23:26.490 --> 00:23:30.170
because I set my alarm for 11:30 instead of 23:30.

592
00:23:30.170 --> 00:23:32.620
And so then I slept through the midnight,

593
00:23:32.620 --> 00:23:34.240
and I woke up just like in a start

594
00:23:34.240 --> 00:23:36.983
like drenched in sweat at 12:20 a.m.

595
00:23:37.888 --> 00:23:40.520
And I'm like fuck.
Disqualified.

596
00:23:40.520 --> 00:23:42.540
And I had to like race to get my shit,

597
00:23:42.540 --> 00:23:44.530
'cause at that point, I knew Adam was coming at four,

598
00:23:44.530 --> 00:23:46.860
so there was pushing the 4:00 a.m. run.

599
00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:49.150
So at that point, that's all off.

600
00:23:49.150 --> 00:23:51.010
So I just ran out the door.

601
00:23:51.010 --> 00:23:52.613
And so then, I stopped kind of,

602
00:23:52.613 --> 00:23:54.320
like my legs started tightening up.

603
00:23:54.320 --> 00:23:55.870
My knees started to hurt a little bit,

604
00:23:55.870 --> 00:23:59.860
but for the most part, my body held up really well.

605
00:23:59.860 --> 00:24:04.110
And you know, it was just a great experience.

606
00:24:04.110 --> 00:24:07.420
Like I have to say, it's the, it's my,

607
00:24:07.420 --> 00:24:09.240
the greatest athletic feat I think

608
00:24:09.240 --> 00:24:10.667
of my lifetime that I've ever done.

609
00:24:10.667 --> 00:24:14.020
That's cool.
And I didn't go into it

610
00:24:14.020 --> 00:24:15.540
thinking that was gonna be the case,

611
00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:17.200
but looking back, I have to say,

612
00:24:17.200 --> 00:24:18.450
I don't think there's anything close,

613
00:24:18.450 --> 00:24:20.690
maybe, I climbed Kilimanjaro, but that's,

614
00:24:20.690 --> 00:24:24.010
I think less people have done this than have climbed Kili.

615
00:24:24.010 --> 00:24:25.240
How would you compare those two?

616
00:24:25.240 --> 00:24:27.130
I mean, it's apples and oranges, but-

617
00:24:27.130 --> 00:24:28.880
This is way more demanding.

618
00:24:28.880 --> 00:24:32.057
If you're in shape, Kili is not a killer.

619
00:24:32.057 --> 00:24:35.700
It's a couple, there's difficult moments

620
00:24:35.700 --> 00:24:39.160
and you're going into altitude, so altitude is a game.

621
00:24:39.160 --> 00:24:41.230
Anybody could suffer from altitude at any time.

622
00:24:41.230 --> 00:24:43.570
So there's some variables

623
00:24:43.570 --> 00:24:47.030
that are beyond your control with altitude.

624
00:24:47.030 --> 00:24:52.030
But if you're in shape, it's not that hard of a thing to do.

625
00:24:54.380 --> 00:24:58.140
But this, 48 miles in two days,

626
00:24:58.140 --> 00:25:00.628
I don't know, is that an ultra or is it

627
00:25:00.628 --> 00:25:01.477
fake ultra.
Anything, I don't know.

628
00:25:01.477 --> 00:25:03.380
It's a fake ultra.
Technically,

629
00:25:03.380 --> 00:25:06.240
an ultra is defined as anything longer than a marathon,

630
00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:10.164
but I don't know when you break it up, how that, who cares?

631
00:25:10.164 --> 00:25:11.000
You know what I mean?
It's like-

632
00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:12.030
You did a hard thing.

633
00:25:12.030 --> 00:25:14.210
If you wanna put a label on it.

634
00:25:14.210 --> 00:25:15.096
You know what it's like?

635
00:25:15.096 --> 00:25:19.050
It's like, the breaking it up made it easier for sure.

636
00:25:19.050 --> 00:25:20.670
No question about it.

637
00:25:20.670 --> 00:25:23.610
And that was the accessibility that you were pointing out,

638
00:25:23.610 --> 00:25:24.650
that's what's so cool about it.

639
00:25:24.650 --> 00:25:26.630
And that it sneaks up on you.

640
00:25:26.630 --> 00:25:27.570
'Cause you're like, I could do,

641
00:25:27.570 --> 00:25:31.680
I could run four miles every four hours, slow.

642
00:25:31.680 --> 00:25:34.320
But then, it incrementally,

643
00:25:34.320 --> 00:25:36.920
the degree of difficulty gets notched up

644
00:25:36.920 --> 00:25:39.980
in a second as the sleep slips away.

645
00:25:39.980 --> 00:25:42.000
The second day is tough, second day is tough.

646
00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:43.910
But like, I think there were a couple moments,

647
00:25:43.910 --> 00:25:46.230
I think after the first 16 miles,

648
00:25:46.230 --> 00:25:49.083
I looked up and I was like, okay, 32 to go.

649
00:25:50.928 --> 00:25:53.340
You try not to check the scoreboard.

650
00:25:53.340 --> 00:25:55.240
But then once you get past the first day-

651
00:25:55.240 --> 00:25:57.260
Just focus on what's right in front of you to do,

652
00:25:57.260 --> 00:25:59.037
which is get through those four miles.

653
00:25:59.037 --> 00:26:03.030
And I rode my bike down on Saturday afternoon

654
00:26:03.030 --> 00:26:05.368
to do a temp check on you.
You did.

655
00:26:05.368 --> 00:26:06.201
And you were in good spirits

656
00:26:06.201 --> 00:26:08.510
and April was out there with you pushing the baby

657
00:26:08.510 --> 00:26:10.670
and you were all smiles and laughs.

658
00:26:10.670 --> 00:26:12.430
Yeah, I mean, I try to keep it that way.

659
00:26:12.430 --> 00:26:14.300
I think, look, a couple of things,

660
00:26:14.300 --> 00:26:17.140
I didn't go into it saying I'm gonna try to do this.

661
00:26:17.140 --> 00:26:20.690
I went into it feeling like I'm going to do this.

662
00:26:20.690 --> 00:26:22.500
So it was never really,

663
00:26:22.500 --> 00:26:23.960
and especially once I realized

664
00:26:23.960 --> 00:26:25.850
how many people were paying attention,

665
00:26:25.850 --> 00:26:26.683
there was no-
Yeah.

666
00:26:26.683 --> 00:26:28.870
There was no graceful bailouts.

667
00:26:28.870 --> 00:26:30.160
Yeah, then it becomes a whole other thing.

668
00:26:30.160 --> 00:26:31.727
Which I did to myself, of course,

669
00:26:31.727 --> 00:26:33.870
but I did it casually without realizing.

670
00:26:33.870 --> 00:26:36.567
Well, that was part of me posting it on Instagram,

671
00:26:36.567 --> 00:26:39.490
and the stories too, it's like, you can't back out, man.

672
00:26:39.490 --> 00:26:40.630
This is like public.

673
00:26:40.630 --> 00:26:41.760
Oh, I never was going to.

674
00:26:41.760 --> 00:26:46.380
And in terms of like the why,

675
00:26:46.380 --> 00:26:49.480
'cause Misty from Nantucket called in,

676
00:26:49.480 --> 00:26:51.410
she's not gonna, we're not gonna play her question,

677
00:26:51.410 --> 00:26:55.000
but her question was basically, did I find a why,

678
00:26:55.000 --> 00:26:56.250
'cause I couldn't answer that.

679
00:26:56.250 --> 00:26:58.060
And she said you scolded me.

680
00:26:58.060 --> 00:26:59.796
And-
Did I scold you?

681
00:26:59.796 --> 00:27:00.980
Well, she's-
I was challenging you.

682
00:27:00.980 --> 00:27:01.980
You challenged me.

683
00:27:03.630 --> 00:27:05.540
So my why, I still didn't really

684
00:27:05.540 --> 00:27:06.690
figure it out in the moment.

685
00:27:06.690 --> 00:27:08.850
Partly because the pacers

686
00:27:08.850 --> 00:27:10.980
take some of that introspection away,

687
00:27:10.980 --> 00:27:12.310
which I don't think is a bad thing.

688
00:27:12.310 --> 00:27:13.520
It's just the way it goes.

689
00:27:13.520 --> 00:27:15.880
When you have a friend next to you in life,

690
00:27:15.880 --> 00:27:18.760
you're just not thinking about yourself as much.

691
00:27:18.760 --> 00:27:23.240
But listening to Steven Pressfield on your podcast

692
00:27:23.240 --> 00:27:26.210
and just thinking about what you guys

693
00:27:26.210 --> 00:27:29.770
were talking about around finding your hero's journey

694
00:27:29.770 --> 00:27:31.897
and going through that hard thing.

695
00:27:31.897 --> 00:27:33.140
And thinking about my life,

696
00:27:33.140 --> 00:27:38.090
which for a long time was defined by travel

697
00:27:38.090 --> 00:27:39.500
and going to different cultures

698
00:27:39.500 --> 00:27:43.400
and being kind of alone in those scenarios,

699
00:27:43.400 --> 00:27:44.640
and having the pandemic come through

700
00:27:44.640 --> 00:27:45.950
and that's completely wiped out,

701
00:27:45.950 --> 00:27:47.633
and then of course life changes.

702
00:27:48.970 --> 00:27:50.830
You know, I do think there was part of me

703
00:27:50.830 --> 00:27:54.703
was just hungry for some tests like that, a test of self.

704
00:27:56.130 --> 00:27:59.690
And so I do think that was like this subconscious pull.

705
00:27:59.690 --> 00:28:00.650
I didn't realize it.

706
00:28:00.650 --> 00:28:03.130
I didn't even think about it when I got her message.

707
00:28:03.130 --> 00:28:06.310
I thought about it listening to you guys talk about it.

708
00:28:06.310 --> 00:28:08.420
And I do think there's something there

709
00:28:08.420 --> 00:28:10.760
that that was a call to action.

710
00:28:10.760 --> 00:28:13.537
And to find out what was inside me, to see if I could do it.

711
00:28:13.537 --> 00:28:14.810
And the fact that I could do it,

712
00:28:14.810 --> 00:28:17.210
stay good humored and stay positive,

713
00:28:17.210 --> 00:28:21.290
I think is a good feeling.

714
00:28:21.290 --> 00:28:23.040
I'm satisfied with that answer.

715
00:28:23.040 --> 00:28:25.500
I'm not gonna scold you on that.

716
00:28:25.500 --> 00:28:28.410
I think what happens is, yeah, you have that pull,

717
00:28:28.410 --> 00:28:31.780
that call to doing something hard, and then you do it.

718
00:28:31.780 --> 00:28:34.150
And what that does is put another brick

719
00:28:34.150 --> 00:28:38.630
in the wall of this identity that you craft

720
00:28:38.630 --> 00:28:40.930
around being somebody who does hard things.

721
00:28:40.930 --> 00:28:44.203
Like I do hard things, I can do hard things.

722
00:28:44.203 --> 00:28:48.710
I'm not afraid of the degree of difficulty

723
00:28:48.710 --> 00:28:53.710
or the unknown of something I've never experienced before.

724
00:28:55.670 --> 00:28:57.330
I welcome that into my life.

725
00:28:57.330 --> 00:29:00.020
And each one of those experiences that you have

726
00:29:00.020 --> 00:29:02.590
is like another notch in the belt

727
00:29:02.590 --> 00:29:06.300
that I think leads to greater self-esteem

728
00:29:06.300 --> 00:29:08.600
and kind of how you navigate the world

729
00:29:08.600 --> 00:29:10.783
in every other facet of your life.

730
00:29:11.910 --> 00:29:14.010
I do feel like I got a self-esteem bump.

731
00:29:14.010 --> 00:29:15.850
Like I felt it, like the next couple,

732
00:29:15.850 --> 00:29:19.423
the day after, I was a bit of a wreck, and I had a deadline.

733
00:29:19.423 --> 00:29:22.010
Did you ever have any like drowsy moments

734
00:29:22.010 --> 00:29:24.295
where you were like short where they roll or like-

735
00:29:24.295 --> 00:29:25.327
No.
No?

736
00:29:25.327 --> 00:29:26.800
No.
That's what happens with me.

737
00:29:26.800 --> 00:29:29.570
Yeah, but I think part of that is I didn't

738
00:29:29.570 --> 00:29:33.040
push myself to the edge in terms of pace.

739
00:29:33.040 --> 00:29:35.363
I was very relaxed in my pace, so-

740
00:29:35.363 --> 00:29:36.930
I was worried about you at one moment,

741
00:29:36.930 --> 00:29:40.290
because you were talking about how your knee was acting up.

742
00:29:40.290 --> 00:29:43.780
I just didn't want you to bury yourself and injure yourself,

743
00:29:43.780 --> 00:29:45.730
because you felt the public pressure

744
00:29:45.730 --> 00:29:47.470
of completing this thing.

745
00:29:47.470 --> 00:29:49.170
Well, I would have kept going.

746
00:29:49.170 --> 00:29:51.810
Even if the knee was a problem, I would have walked.

747
00:29:51.810 --> 00:29:52.850
I wasn't gonna stop.

748
00:29:52.850 --> 00:29:57.590
So when you work with David Goggins,

749
00:29:57.590 --> 00:29:59.790
you don't stop when your knee cries a little bit.

750
00:29:59.790 --> 00:30:02.799
And since you are working with David Goggins,

751
00:30:02.799 --> 00:30:06.100
you couldn't have not done this anyway, right?

752
00:30:06.100 --> 00:30:07.330
Well, I could have, I mean-
I mean,

753
00:30:07.330 --> 00:30:09.060
David didn't put any pressure on you personally.

754
00:30:09.060 --> 00:30:11.110
No, I brought it up to him,

755
00:30:11.110 --> 00:30:12.520
and then once I brought it up to him,

756
00:30:12.520 --> 00:30:14.690
it was basically a done deal.
Then it's over with.

757
00:30:14.690 --> 00:30:16.910
(both laughs)

758
00:30:16.910 --> 00:30:21.440
But he was really, I mean, let's put it this way.

759
00:30:21.440 --> 00:30:23.550
In this time where everyone's so polarized

760
00:30:23.550 --> 00:30:26.420
and at each other's throats, he creates this thing.

761
00:30:26.420 --> 00:30:28.590
Some 30,000 people around-
Is that how many people?

762
00:30:28.590 --> 00:30:31.903
Yeah, it's like in the 20 to 30 range and closer to 30,

763
00:30:32.740 --> 00:30:34.560
signed up to do this thing,

764
00:30:34.560 --> 00:30:36.730
this really hard thing altogether.

765
00:30:36.730 --> 00:30:38.320
There's a real feeling of unity

766
00:30:38.320 --> 00:30:42.090
among the people that were doing it, comradery.

767
00:30:42.090 --> 00:30:47.090
And it just shows what, if you focus in versus focusing out,

768
00:30:47.720 --> 00:30:51.381
the positive repercussions are, I think,

769
00:30:51.381 --> 00:30:54.670
impossible to calculate, and it's just a ripple effect.

770
00:30:54.670 --> 00:30:58.410
And so kudos to David for recognizing that,

771
00:30:58.410 --> 00:30:59.763
seeing that, leading that.

